  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM EST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nThe current Day Cloud Phase RGB shows patchy high-level clouds \nacross the area and a scattering of lower-level clouds over the \nKeweenaw. Temperatures are in the upper 20s to low 30s with \ngenerally light wind. A shortwave upstream of our area is supplying \nmoisture for slightly more dense high-level cloud cover over \nportions of Wisconsin and Minnesota, which will make its way over \nthe U.P. tonight.\n\nAnomalously high 500mb heights (95th-98th percentile in the LREF) \ncontinue through Thursday morning. Very dry air at the lower levels \n(mean 925mb RHs of 15-25%) combined with partial high-level cloud \ncover will allow temperatures to fall into the single digits to low \ntwenties across the U.P. tonight, with warmest conditions along the \nlakeshore and coldest in the interior west. As the surface ridge \ncurrently over the area progresses eastward Wednesday, light surface \nwinds back southeasterly. This will keep the eastern half of the \nforecast area warmer and moister than the west thanks to the \ninfluence of Lake Michigan (temperatures and dewpoints in the mid-\n20s versus in the low teens west).\n\nThanksgiving Day sees temperatures warming into the low 40s thanks \nto continued warm air advection at the surface. Dry weather is \nexpected throughout the day despite increasing cloud cover. Thursday \nnight into Friday, surface ridging gives way to falling pressure as \na low pressure system makes its way north along the East Coast. \nPrecipitation is possible (10-20% chance) Friday for southern \nMenominee and eastern Luce and Schoolcraft Counties, although totals \nare expected to remain below a tenth of an inch (less than a 25% \nchance of exceedance) in these areas. At this time, precipitation \nlooks to remain mainly liquid despite surface temperatures falling \nbelow freezing Friday night as 925mb through 850mb temperatures are \nlikely to remain above freezing (80% chance) per the latest GFS and \nLREF guidance.\n\nHeading into the weekend, an Alberta Clipper system swings through \nto the north of Great Lakes. This increases the chances of the \n540dam 500mb isobar moving back over the U.P. (~65% chance) as \ntemperatures at 850mb and 925mb cool back below freezing Saturday \nnight through Sunday. Chances for lake effect precipitation along \nthe lakeshore increase to 10-40% Saturday through Monday as winds \nstrengthen and shift westerly, then northerly. Exact placement and \ntiming of heaviest precipitation is still uncertain at this time.",
